391 0 obj
<<
/P 930 0 R
/S /Figure
/Alt (The image consists of two graphs and a caption. \(graph 1\). A curve and a rectangle are graphed above a horizontal line. Two points are labeled on the line. They are as follows from left to right: x = a and x = b. The curve begins at a point vertically above x = a on top left of the viewing window, goes down and to the right with increasing steepness, after a point goes further down and to the right with decreasing steepness, and ends at a point vertically above x = b. The region under the curve till the horizontal line is shaded. The left and right boundaries of the shaded region are x = a and x = b respectively. The shaded region is labeled plane region. The rectangle is labeled representative rectangle. It is graphed between the curve and the horizontal line in the shaded region. The upper side of the rectangle intersects the curve. The lower side of the rectangle lies on the horizontal line. The distance between the upper and lower sides of the rectangle is labeled R and the distance between the left and right sides of the rectangle is labeled Delta x. R > Delta x. \(graph 2\). A horizontal line is labeled axis of revolution. A solid of revolution and a representative disk in the solid of revolution are graphed on the left side of the axis of revolution that passes through their center. The solid of revolution and the representative disk are obtained by revolving the plane region and the representative rectangle in graph 1 about the axis of revolution. The width of the representative disk is Delta x. Six adjacent disks of the same width are arranged on the right side of the axis of revolution that passes through their centers. The disks are arranged with increasing order of their radii from left to right. It is the approximation of the solid of revolution by n disks. An arrow indicates a counterclockwise rotation of the axis of revolution. Caption. Disk method.)
/Type /StructElem
/K [46]
/Pg 17 0 R
>>
endobj
