631 0 obj
<<
/P 942 0 R
/S /Figure
/Alt (Two graphs. \(graph 1\). A curve and two rectangles are graphed in the first quadrant of the x y coordinate plane. The curve resembles the right half of an upward opening parabola. It begins at \(0, 1\) on the positive y axis, goes up and to the right, and ends at the labeled point \(1, 2\). The region under the curve till the positive x axis is shaded. The left and right boundaries of the shaded region are x = 0 and x = 1 respectively. The shaded region is labeled plane region. One rectangle is graphed between the curve and the vertical line x = 1 in the shaded region. The left side of the rectangle intersects the curve. The right side of the rectangle lies on the vertical line x = 1. The distance between the positive y axis and the left side of the rectangle is labeled r. The distance between the positive y axis and the right side of the rectangle is labeled R. The distance between the upper and lower sides of the rectangle is labeled Delta y. R minus r > Delta y. The second rectangle is graphed under the curve in the shaded region. The left side of the rectangle lies on the positive y axis. The right side of the rectangle lies on the vertical line x = 1. The distance between the upper and lower sides of the rectangle is labeled Delta y. For 1 <= y <= 2: R = 1 and r = sqrt\(y minus 1\). For 0 <= y <= 1: R = 1 and r = 0. An arrow indicates a counterclockwise rotation of the y axis. \(graph 2\). A solid of revolution is graphed on the x y coordinate plane. The solid of revolution is obtained by revolving the plane region in graph 1 about the y axis. An arrow indicates a counterclockwise rotation of the y axis.)
/Type /StructElem
/K [20]
/Pg 29 0 R
>>
endobj
